{
  "answer_text": "Yes, retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) changes the latency of LLM-generated answers, with the direction of the change varying by dataset and specific implementation. The evidence shows that RAG can either increase or decrease latency compared to parametric-only or closed-book models.\n\n1. **Increased Latency:**\n   - On the **GeoFacts** dataset, the retrieve-then-read model had a latency of **3.21 seconds**, which is higher than the parametric-only model's latency of **1.57 seconds** [S004, 2568-2901].\n   - On the **HistoryQuest** dataset, the retrieve-then-read model had a latency of **2.63 seconds**, which is higher than the no-retrieval baseline's latency of **1.99 seconds** [S019, 2114-2579].\n\n2. **Decreased Latency:**\n   - On the **TriviaCheck** dataset, the retrieve-then-read model had a latency of **1.09 seconds**, which is lower than the no-retrieval baseline's latency of **1.91 seconds** [S019, 2114-2579].\n   - On the **TriviaCheck** dataset, the retrieval-augmented model had a latency of **0.94 seconds**, which is lower than the closed-book model's latency of **1.96 seconds** [S014, 1788-2126].\n\n3. **Negligible Change:**\n   - On the **BioClaimQA** dataset, the retrieval-augmented model had a latency of **1.12 seconds**, which is very close to the closed-book model's latency of **1.16 seconds** [S014, 1788-2126].\n   - On the **LawFactQA** dataset, the retrieve-then-read model had a latency of **3.47 seconds**, which is higher than the parametric-only model's latency of **2.74 seconds** [S004, 2568-2901].\n\nIn summary, RAG does change latency, but the magnitude and direction depend on the specific dataset and system configuration. The latency is measured as the mean seconds needed to produce one answer [S004, 1585-1880, S014, 1196-1629, S019, 1446-1942].",
